BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-07_1758_facebook_why-ai-agent-pilots-stall.md@social-squad-sche
 dule
DTSTAMP:20261007T095800Z
DTSTART:20261007T095800Z
DTEND:20261007T102800Z
SUMMARY:Facebook: Why most AI agent pilots stall\, and the boring setup tha
 t got mine past week one
DESCRIPTION:approved - Most company pilots of AI agents never make it into 
 daily use. That's the message from nearly every write-up I read this month
 . The reasons they give are rarely about the technology. They're scope tha
 t keeps growing\, messy data\, and nobody deciding who checks the output.\
 n\nI don't run a company pilot. I run a one-person setup: three agents (re
 search\, content\, scheduling) and a dashboard I built in a single session
 . It's small and it's pre-revenue. But it's real\, and I've been using it 
 since. So I went back through it and asked which choices kept it from stal
 ling. Three stood out.\n\n**Each agent got one job.**\n\nThe temptation wa
 s to build one clever assistant that does everything. I did the opposite. 
 The researcher only researches. The content agent only drafts. The schedul
 er only handles timing. They can't wander into each other's work\, which a
 lso means that when something goes wrong\, I know where to look.\n\n**The 
 data came first.**\n\nBefore any agent touched my reference material\, I s
 pent time sorting it. 83 files\, each classified so an agent can find the 
 right one instead of guessing. It was the least exciting part of the whole
  build. It's also the part I'd skip next time if I were rushing\, and I'd 
 regret it\, because an agent working from a pile of unsorted files gives y
 ou confident nonsense.\n\n**Nothing moves without me.**\n\nEvery handoff h
 as a defined shape\, and every piece of content sits in a queue until I ap
 prove it. This is the governance piece\, and mine is tiny: one person\, on
 e approval button. Still\, it changes the question from "can I trust the a
 gent?" to "did I look at this?" The second one I can answer.\n\nWhat I can
 't tell you is whether this holds up at scale. A team of forty with real b
 udgets and conflicting priorities is a different problem\, and one session
  of building doesn't prove anything about it. I also can't give you a clea
 n failure rate for pilots\, because the numbers I found disagree with each
  other. "Most stall" is as far as I'll go.\n\nBut the pattern matches what
  I saw at my own desk. The agents that would have failed were the ones I'd
  have made too broad\, fed with a mess\, and left unchecked. Narrow\, fed 
 properly\, reviewed: that's it. Nothing clever.\n\nIf you're about to star
 t an agent project at work\, try this first. Write down the one job. Then 
 write down who approves the output. If you can't answer either in a senten
 ce\, the pilot is already in trouble.\n\nCheers!
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
